# The target architecture after these fixes

```text
             Letta App Server
            one managed process
                    │
                    ▼
            runtime-letta
          one singleton client
                    │
           fresh session/run
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          │                   │
      Write/Edit            stream
          │                   │
          ▼                   ▼
 Campaign Git dir        result terminal
          │                   │
          └─────────┬─────────┘
                    ▼
                session.close
                    │
                    ▼
              WorkerExecution
                    │
                    ▼
             WorkerKit receipt
                    │
                    ▼
               freeze run
                    │
                    ▼
                 Harbor
                    │
            ┌───────┴───────┐
            ▼               ▼
       Assessor v0      regrade v1/v2/v3
            │               │
            └───────┬───────┘
                    ▼
                  Hydra
```

That is the checkpoint we wanted.

The most important thing is that **the timeout does not currently indicate that MiMo or Letta is intrinsically too slow**. The trace is dominated by integration defects: the worker has no mutation tools, your stream doesn't have a real timeout, terminal results aren't handled, sessions aren't closed, and each local client can leave another App Server behind. Letta itself has an open upstream issue confirming the client-owned local App Server lifecycle problem.

Once those are fixed, I would expect the tiny deterministic file-write smoke test to tell us very quickly whether there is any deeper model/provider issue. And after the first successful real WorkerRun, **Harbor regrade means you should need dramatically fewer full Letta runs**, which is exactly the development-speed unlock we were looking for.

